<!--
  An invented four months, built for one situation the automatic resolver
  cannot get right on its own: a merchant whose terminal prints two different
  names.

  Seven entries, and by the resolver's own reading three counterparties:

    TESTFUEL            two visits, January and March
    TEST FUEL STATION   two visits, February and April
    TESTBOEKHANDEL      three visits, monthly

  The first two are one filling station. Nothing in the statement says so —
  there is no shared IBAN, no mandate and no similarity a program should be
  trusted to act on — so AYQ does not guess it. A person says it, once, and
  that is what an alias is.

  Read as three, neither half of the filling station recurs: two visits is a
  coincidence. Read as one, four visits a month apart are a rhythm. That is the
  difference an alias makes, and it is what the recurring test asserts.

  Every name, IBAN, terminal and amount here belongs to nobody.
-->
